Instructions for AI
Explain why, never what. Every step here was taken for a reason, and the
reason is the interesting part. Give your reader that — what was being
protected, what was let go of, and why it mattered enough to do at all.
They should leave understanding the idea, not the mechanics.
The message holds what was done. The note attached to it holds why. Where
there is a note, that is the one you want.

This commit's message is a subject line and nothing else, so everything that
made it worth doing lives here.

Two rulings, and both are corrections to something that had already been
agreed.

The first retires a guard that had been ratified on a premise measurement did
not support. `MAX_DECODE_PIXELS` was accepted on the understanding that it
saved roughly half the peak memory of an image attachment. It does not, and
the reason is structural: the canvas is created after the longest-edge early
return and is sized to the target, so it can never grow to more than about ten
megabytes — against a decode that can reach a hundred and sixty. The guard
could not prevent the allocation that is the actual risk, and it stood in front
of it looking as though it could.

Its cost was also inverted, which is what settled the question. Images past the
threshold skipped optimisation altogether, so the largest files — the ones with
the most to gain — received the least help. A guard that protects nothing and
penalises the cases it is nominally there for is worse than no guard, because
it also stops anyone looking for a real one. `DOWNSCALE_TIMEOUT_MS` bounds the
pathological case honestly, by wall clock, and makes no claim it cannot keep.

Code that claims a protection it does not provide is the same class of defect
as a docblock describing behaviour the function does not have — and this lane
began with one of those.

The second ruling repairs damage done by a correct finding. The legibility
floor was right: below a certain shorter edge, resizing produces something
unreadable, so the image should not be resized. But most uploads on this
product are screenshots, screenshots are tall, and a tall image's shorter edge
is the one that trips the floor — so the effect of an entirely sound catch was
that the most common upload received no optimisation at all.

So the floor now governs only dimension reduction. Below it a PNG is still
re-encoded to lossless WebP at its native size, which on the tested files takes
a multi-megabyte upload down to somewhere between one and two megabytes without
touching a pixel. Peak canvas rises from roughly ten megabytes to about
fifty-six on a fourteen-megapixel source, which is survivable on anything made
in the last five years, and a failure still falls back to the original file
rather than to a worse one.

What stays unmitigated, deliberately: an out-of-memory failure on a genuinely
pathological source. That waits on parsing dimensions from the file header
before any decode is attempted, which is a larger change than this was, and it
is docketed rather than quietly absorbed. Saying so is the point — an
unmitigated risk that is written down is a decision, and one that is not is an
accident waiting to be discovered by whoever hits it.
